      "content": "2.1 Physiographic Divisions"
    },
    {
      "id": "oby0new",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "lq0hjiy",
      "src": "/books/Guide/Odisha CONSTABLE/images/image_lq0hjiy.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Odisha has four major physiographic regions:",
      "id": "qi7sb13"
    },
    {
      "id": "7rtn97j",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "The Northern Plateau (Chota Nagpur Plateau extension)",
        "The Central Table Land (Eastern Ghats &amp; hill ranges)",
        "The Valley &amp; Rolling Upland Region",
        "The Coastal Plains (Delta region)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "afbek8f",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2.1.1 Northern Plateau"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The northern plateau region forms the southward extension of the Chota Nagpur Plateau, covering districts like Sundargarh, Jharsuguda, Sambalpur, Keonjhar, and Mayurbhanj. This region is rich in minerals (iron ore, coal, manganese). The Simlipal Hills are in this zone.",
      "id": "opu1v09"
    },
    {
      "id": "iefxzpy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2.1.2 Eastern Ghats"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Eastern Ghats run through the central and southern parts of Odisha. They are discontinuous hills running NE–SW. Important hill ranges include Mahendragiri (highest peak, 1501 m), Deomali (highest peak of Eastern Ghats at 1672 m – in Koraput district), and Niyamgiri Hills (Rayagada/Kalahandi).",
      "id": "q8chp3m"
    },
